language=EN}}</ref> He has also stated that non-viewpoint characters [[Catelyn Stark|Lady Stoneheart]], Quaithe, [[Euron Greyjoy]] and [[Rickon Stark]] will appear.<ref name="notablog7822"/> He stated that [[Melisandre]] will return as viewpoint but did not state which novel her chapters will appear in.<ref>[https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zmJMbDzH_NA JUEGOS DE TRONOS de George R.R Martin en la FIL de Guadalajara 2016] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20161206013020/https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zmJMbDzH_NA|date=December 6, 2016}} Event occurs at 27:22</ref> He teased at the 2014 [[San Diego Comic-Con]] that Jeyne Westerling, Robb Stark's widow, will appear in the prologue chapter, but did not reveal who will be the POV character.<ref>{{Cite web |date=July 28, 2014 |title=George R.R. Martin teases the 'The Winds of Winter' at SDCC |url=https://www.hypable.com/george-r-r-martin-teases-the-prologue-and-plotlines-of-the-winds-of-winter/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://archive.today/20151105011438/http://www.hypable.com/george-r-r-martin-teases-the-prologue-and-plotlines-of-the-winds-of-winter/ |archive-date=November 5, 2015 |access-date=November 11, 2019 |website=Hypable}}</ref>

==Publication history==
===Structure and length===
''The Winds of Winter''<ref name="dancingincircles"/> was originally intended, in the very early stages of the series, to be the final installment of ''A Song of Ice and Fire'' (then conceived as a trilogy).<ref name="atlantic_sex_fantasy" /> Following his expansion of the series, Martin eventually concluded it would be succeeded by one final novel, ''A Dream of Spring''.<ref name="grrm_iv_infinityplus" />

Martin believes the last two volumes of the series will be works of more than 1,500 manuscript pages each.<ref>Martin, George R.R. (May 20, 2019). [http://georgerrmartin.com/notablog/2019/05/20/an-ending/ "An Ending"] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190521220322/http://georgerrmartin.com/notablog/2019/05/20/an-ending/ |date=May 21, 2019 }}. Not A Blog.</ref><ref name="empire_webchat" /> He does not intend to separate characters by geography again, as he was forced to do with ''[[A Feast for Crows]]'' because of the unpublishable length of that novel's original manuscript. But, as he stated in a 2011 interview, "Three years from now when I'm sitting on 1,800 pages of manuscript with no end in sight, who the hell knows".<ref name="grmm_iv_ew" /> In 2018, he revealed that some of his publishers had suggested splitting ''The Winds of Winter'' into two books but that he was "resisting that notion".<ref>{{Cite web |title=FIRE & BLOOD : On The Way|publisher=Not A Blog|url=http://georgerrmartin.com/notablog/2018/04/25/fire-blood-on-the-way/#comment-706 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180425182310/http://georgerrmartin.com/notablog/2018/04/25/fire-blood-on-the-way/#comment-706 |archive-date=April 25, 2018 |access-date=April 25, 2018 |website=georgerrmartin.com}}</ref>
In 2022, he stated that ''The Winds of Winter'' could be bigger than ''A Storm of Swords'' or ''A Dance with Dragons'';<ref>{{Cite web |title=Random Bits of This and That &#124;|publisher=Not A Blog|url=https://georgerrmartin.com/notablog/2022/04/29/random-bits-of-this-and-that/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220429215411/https://georgerrmartin.com/notablog/2022/04/29/random-bits-of-this-and-that/ |archive-date=April 29, 2022 |access-date=April 30, 2022}}</ref> the manuscript for ''A Storm of Swords'' had 1,521 pages<ref name="LJ 2009-10-06">{{Cite web |last=Martin |first=George R. R. |date=October 6, 2009 |title=Not A Blog: Dance, Dance, Dance |url=http://grrm.livejournal.com/113041.html |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20091228025320/http://grrm.livejournal.com/113041.html |archive-date=December 28, 2009 |access-date=March 4, 2010 |publisher=GRRM.Livejournal.com (Author's LiveJournal blog)}}</ref> and the manuscript for ''A Dance with Dragons'' exceeded 1,600 pages before trimming.<ref name="http://grrm.livejournal.com/204875.html">{{Cite web |date=March 27, 2011 |title=KONG |url=http://grrm.livejournal.com/204875.html |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://archive.today/20121128035210/http://grrm.livejournal.com/204875.html |archive-date=November 28, 2012 |access-date=June 30, 2011|publisher=Not A Blog}}</ref>

===Confirmed chapters ===
By June 2010, Martin had finished four chapters for ''The Winds of Winter'' from the viewpoints of [[Sansa Stark]], [[Arya Stark]], and [[Arianne Martell]].<ref name="dancingincircles"/> In July 2010, he added an [[Aeron Greyjoy]] chapter that had been moved from ''A Dance with Dragons'' to ''The Winds of Winter'', accumulating around 100 completed manuscript pages.<ref name="grrm_blog_200721"/> Following the publication of ''A Dance with Dragons'' in July 2011, Martin returned to writing the series in January 2012,<ref name="ew_fantasy_king"/> having spent the intervening time on his U.S. and overseas book publicity tours and attending various conventions.<ref name="indigo_iv1"/>

In December 2011, Martin posted a [[Theon Greyjoy]] viewpoint chapter from ''The Winds of Winter''; he also included another sample chapter at the end of the North American paperback version of ''A Dance with Dragons'',<ref name="smartertravel"/><ref name="sample chapter"/> which was originally expected to be released in mid-2012,<ref name="smartertravel"/> but was released on October 29, 2013.<ref>{{Cite web |title=A Dance with Dragons by George R. R. Martin |url=http://www.randomhouse.com/book/108334/a-dance-with-dragons-by-george-rr-martin |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20130126181000/http://www.randomhouse.com/book/108334/a-dance-with-dragons-by-george-rr-martin |archive-date=January 26, 2013 |access-date=February 16, 2013 |publisher=Random House}}</ref> (International paperback editions of ''A Dance with Dragons'' published a year earlier did not include an unpublished sample chapter.)<ref name="amazon_pubpp_adwd"/> In the first quarter of 2012, Martin read new chapters of other characters at public events, including the chapters of [[Victarion Greyjoy]] and [[Tyrion Lannister]].<ref name="harpvoy_eastercon"/> Martin continued work editing anthologies and completing a large, highly detailed series [[atlas]] ''[[The Lands of Ice and Fire]]'', which was published in October 2012.<ref name="ustream_eastercon12"/><ref name="randomhouse_lands"/> Martin published another sample chapter from Arianne Martell's POV on his website in January 2013.<ref name="grrm_blog_130108"/> On March 27, ten days before the [[Game of Thrones (season 4)|''Game of Thrones'' season four]] premiere, Martin posted a chapter on his website, titled ''Mercy''. Martin said the new chapter is actually an old one, though never published nor publicly read.<ref name="hollywoodlife">{{Cite web|last=Tierney |first=H.L. |title='Winds Of Winter' Chapter — George RR Martin Releases 'Game Of Thrones' Excerpt |url=http://hollywoodlife.com/2014/03/27/winds-of-winter-chapter-george-rr-martin-game-of-thrones/ |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140328141155/http://hollywoodlife.com/2014/03/27/winds-of-winter-chapter-george-rr-martin-game-of-thrones/ |archive-date=March 28, 2014 |access-date=March 28, 2014 |website=[[Hollywood Life]]|date=March 27, 2014}}</ref> In April 2015, Martin posted a Sansa Stark viewpoint chapter from ''The Winds of Winter'' titled ''Alayne''.<ref name="alayne"/> In May 2016, Martin replaced this chapter with an Arianne Martell chapter he had read from at Mysticon.<ref name="livejournal.com"/>

{{as of|2016|6|post=,}} a total of 11 chapters from ''The Winds of Winter'' had been either read publicly or published as sample chapters. The point of view characters with released material are Victarion, Aeron, Tyrion, Barristan, Arianne, Theon, Mercy (Arya), and Alayne (Sansa).<ref name="NME-release">{{Cite news |date=January 15, 2017 |title=The Winds of Winter: release date, news, plot, chapters and returning characters |language=en-US |work=NME |url=https://www.nme.com/blogs/tv-blogs/the-winds-of-winter-release-date-chapters-1942288 |url-status=live |access-date=January 17, 2017 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170115202213/http://www.nme.com/blogs/tv-blogs/the-winds-of-winter-release-date-chapters-1942288 |archive-date=January 15, 2017}}</ref> In 2018, Martin indicated he would not release any more sample chapters.<ref>{{Cite web |title=FIRE & BLOOD : On The Way|publisher=Not A Blog|url=http://georgerrmartin.com/notablog/2018/04/25/fire-blood-on-the-way/#comments |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180425182310/http://georgerrmartin.com/notablog/2018/04/25/fire-blood-on-the-way/#comments |archive-date=April 25, 2018 |access-date=April 25, 2018 |website=georgerrmartin.com}}</ref> In 2020, he indicated he had been revising some of the sample chapters.<ref name="Nov2020">{{Cite web |title=Back to Westeros &#124|publisher=Not A Blog|url=https://georgerrmartin.com/notablog/2020/11/08/back-to-westeros/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201109074733/https://georgerrmartin.com/notablog/2020/11/08/back-to-westeros/ |archive-date=November 9, 2020 |access-date=November 10, 2020}}</ref>

